          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>How does the carbapenem-resistance determinant NDM-1 spread between different ba</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>How does the carbapenem-resistance determinant NDM-1 spread between different bacterial species within a hospital?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Through spontaneous chromosomal mutations during binary fission</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Chromosomal mutations produce resistance within a single organism's lineage but cannot transfer resistance genes to a different species.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Through horizontal transfer of plasmids carrying the resistance gene</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>NDM-1 is carried on mobile plasmids that can hop between entirely different bacterial species — this horizontal gene transfer is why it spread globally so rapidly after first being identified in New Delhi.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Through clonal expansion of a single resistant lineage</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Clonal spread passes one resistant organism from patient to patient (usually on hands), but it occurs within the same species and does not explain inter-species spread of resistance.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Through bacteriophage-mediated transduction</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Transduction is a real mechanism of gene transfer, but NDM-1 is characteristically carried on conjugative plasmids, which is what makes its inter-species spread so efficient.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The narration distinguishes two modes of resistance spread: clonal spread (same organism, patient to patient via hands) and horizontal gene transfer (resistance genes hop between different species via plasmids). NDM-1 spreads through the latter."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! Plasmid-mediated horizontal gene transfer allows resistance genes like NDM-1 to jump between entirely different bacterial species, making this mechanism far more dangerous than clonal spread alone."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
