          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>In hospital water surveillance, Escherichia coli and total coliforms are tested </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In hospital water surveillance, Escherichia coli and total coliforms are tested as indicator organisms primarily because their presence signals:</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Biofilm formation in the hospital water distribution system</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Biofilms are a concern in hospital plumbing (especially for Legionella), but E. coli and coliforms are not biofilm indicators — they specifically signal faecal contamination.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Faecal contamination of the water supply</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>E. coli and total coliforms are enteric organisms whose presence in water indicates faecal contamination. The standard is strict: zero E. coli per 100 mL of drinking water.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Legionella proliferation in cooling towers</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Legionella is tested separately using culture or PCR, particularly in cooling towers and hot water systems. E. coli and coliforms serve a different purpose as faecal contamination markers.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Chemical contamination from hospital effluent</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Chemical contamination requires separate analytical methods. E. coli and coliforms are biological indicators specifically for faecal contamination of water.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "E. coli and total coliforms are enteric organisms used as indicators because their presence in water signals faecal contamination. Membrane filtration is the most sensitive detection method, and the standard demands zero E. coli per 100 mL of drinking water."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! E. coli and total coliforms are classic faecal indicator organisms. Their detection in hospital drinking water signals faecal contamination, and the standard demands zero E. coli per 100 mL."
                }
              ],
